The Most Dangerous Step on a Construction Site May Be the One Nobody Questions
A construction worker climbs a scaffold to complete a routine installation. The team has performed the same activity dozens of times. The work permit is approved, the supervisor has completed the morning briefing, and everyone believes the risks are understood.

But something has changed.

An edge protection barrier was temporarily removed to allow material movement. A scaffold platform was modified during the previous shift. A worker's fall-arrest connection is not properly secured.

Each issue may appear small in isolation. Together, they can create the conditions for a serious fall.

This is one of the biggest challenges in construction safety: working-at-height risks are dynamic, while many safety management processes remain periodic and document-driven.

As construction projects become larger, more complex and increasingly dependent on multiple contractors, safety teams need better ways to identify changing conditions before someone gets hurt.

AI-powered safety technologies, combined with structured digital EHS management, offer an opportunity to strengthen that process.

What Is AI-Powered Fall Prevention in Construction?
AI-powered fall prevention uses technologies such as computer vision, predictive analytics, digital risk assessments and intelligent safety monitoring to help identify working-at-height hazards, prioritise interventions and support safer construction activities.

Depending on the system and deployment, these technologies may help safety teams recognise missing protective equipment, detect unsafe access conditions, identify recurring high-risk activities and monitor whether corrective actions have been completed.

However, AI does not physically prevent a fall.

Effective fall prevention still depends on engineering controls, properly designed access systems, competent workers, reliable equipment, supervision and emergency preparedness.

AI adds value by helping organisations recognise and respond to hazards earlier.

Why Working-at-Height Safety Remains a Major Construction Challenge
Construction sites rarely remain unchanged for long.

Scaffolding is assembled, modified and dismantled. Temporary platforms are installed. Floor openings appear as work progresses. Cranes move materials between levels, and multiple subcontractors operate in overlapping work zones.

A control that was adequate yesterday may no longer be adequate today.

Common working-at-height hazards include:

Unprotected roof edges and floor openings.

Incomplete or improperly modified scaffolding.

Unsafe ladders and temporary access arrangements.

Incorrectly selected or connected fall-arrest equipment.

Poorly controlled work near fragile surfaces.

Falling tools and materials that endanger workers below.

Inadequate rescue arrangements following a fall-arrest event.

These risks are not solved by increasing paperwork alone.

They require a reliable process for recognising hazards, assigning responsibility, implementing controls and verifying that the work environment remains safe.

From Reactive Safety Management to Proactive Risk Prevention
Traditional construction safety programmes often depend on scheduled inspections, toolbox meetings, manual permit approvals and incident investigations.

These practices remain essential, but their effectiveness can be limited when information moves slowly between workers, supervisors, contractors and corporate safety teams.

Consider a typical construction project.

A safety officer identifies missing edge protection on Level 12 and records the observation. The responsible contractor receives the instruction later. Meanwhile, another crew arrives to begin installation work in the same area.

The problem is not necessarily that the hazard was missed.

The problem is that the information did not reach the right people quickly enough.

Digital EHS systems can help close this communication gap by supporting faster reporting, responsibility assignment, corrective-action tracking and management visibility.

Where suitable AI capabilities are available, they can also assist with hazard classification, trend identification and prioritisation.

The objective is straightforward: move safety information closer to the moment when a decision must be made.

Five Ways AI Can Strengthen Working-at-Height Safety
1. Identifying unsafe conditions before work begins
Working-at-height activities should begin with a suitable risk assessment that considers the task, location, access arrangements, weather, equipment and potential consequences of a fall.

AI-assisted assessment tools may help safety professionals identify overlooked hazards or recommend additional considerations based on approved safety procedures and previous observations.

For example, a planned roofing activity might require reviewing fragile surfaces, weather exposure, edge protection, safe access and rescue arrangements.

The final assessment and approval must remain with competent personnel.

2. Supporting smarter permit-to-work decisions
High-risk work often requires formal authorisation, particularly where site procedures mandate permits for working at height.

A digital permit-to-work process can connect task descriptions, risk assessments, equipment checks, contractor information and approval responsibilities.

Intelligent rules may flag missing prerequisites, expired training records or incomplete safety inspections before authorisation.

For a large EPC project, this can provide better consistency across multiple work fronts.

Importantly, permit approval must reflect actual site conditions. A digitally approved permit is not evidence that a work area is physically safe.

3. Improving visibility through computer vision
AI-enabled cameras and image-analysis systems may be used to detect certain observable safety conditions, such as the presence of helmets, visible harnesses or workers approaching designated restricted areas.

Such systems require careful configuration and validation.

A camera may recognise that a worker is wearing a harness, but it may not reliably determine whether the harness is correctly fitted, connected to a suitable anchor or appropriate for the task.

Computer vision should therefore supplement competent inspection and supervision rather than replace them.

4. Learning from near misses and recurring hazards
A single near miss can reveal a problem. Hundreds of observations can reveal a pattern.

AI-assisted analysis may help identify recurring issues across construction projects, including repeated scaffold defects, incomplete barricading, poor housekeeping around access routes or recurring permit deviations.

These findings can help HSE leaders direct inspections, training and preventive interventions toward higher-priority activities.

The quality of these insights depends on accurate reporting, consistent hazard classifications and representative data.

5. Accelerating corrective-action closure
Identifying a hazard is only the beginning.

A working-at-height observation should have a clear owner, an appropriate control, a target completion date and evidence of verification.

Digital workflows can support reminders, escalation, photographic evidence and closure tracking.

For critical hazards, the priority is immediate risk control, including stopping affected work when necessary. A software notification should never substitute for urgent on-site intervention.

Why the Hierarchy of Controls Must Come Before AI
Technology is most effective when it supports sound safety engineering.

For working at height, organisations should first consider whether the activity can be eliminated or performed from ground level.

Where work at height cannot be avoided, suitable collective protection measures—such as properly designed guardrails, protected platforms and other engineered solutions—should generally take priority over reliance on personal protective equipment alone, subject to applicable regulations and task-specific risk assessment.

Personal fall protection, including restraint and fall-arrest systems, requires appropriate selection, anchorage, inspection, training and rescue planning.

Illustrative hierarchy for working-at-height risk control

1

Eliminate work at height

Prefabrication, ground-level assembly or alternative methods

2

Provide collective protection

Guardrails, protected platforms and engineered access

3

Use suitable personal protection

Restraint or fall-arrest systems with rescue arrangements

4

Strengthen administrative controls

Risk assessments, permits, inspections and supervision

5

Add digital and AI-enabled assurance

Monitoring, reporting, analytics and corrective-action visibility

AI is an additional assurance layer, not a substitute for higher-order controls. The sequence is illustrative and must be adapted to the applicable regulatory hierarchy.

A company can deploy advanced analytics and still have an unsafe site if physical fall protection is missing.

The goal is to combine reliable engineering controls with better operational information.

