A Practical Construction Scenario: Managing Work at Height Across Multiple Contractors
Imagine a large commercial development where civil, mechanical, electrical and façade contractors operate simultaneously.

The project safety team needs to coordinate rooftop activities, temporary scaffolds, elevated work platforms and open-floor penetrations.

A digitally connected process could work as follows.

Before work: The contractor submits a task-specific risk assessment and the required permit documentation. The supervisor checks access arrangements, collective protection, worker competency and rescue preparedness.

During work: Safety officers record inspections and observations against the relevant location, activity and contractor. Critical hazards are communicated immediately, and affected work is suspended where required.

After a hazard is identified: The responsible team implements the necessary controls, attaches supporting evidence and requests verification.

At management level: Project leaders review overdue critical actions, repeated hazard categories and contractor performance trends.

Where AI-based analytical capabilities are implemented and validated, historical data may also help identify patterns that deserve additional management attention.

