Responsible AI Adoption in Construction Safety
Before deploying AI-assisted fall-prevention technologies, construction companies should ask several important questions.

How accurately does the system detect the hazards it claims to recognise? How are false alerts managed? Can supervisors explain and challenge the system's recommendations? Who is responsible for final safety decisions?

For camera-based systems, organisations must also address worker privacy, data retention, access controls and applicable employment and data-protection requirements.

Any AI system used in a safety-critical process should undergo appropriate testing, site-specific validation and ongoing performance review.

Human accountability must remain central to every safety-critical decision.

The Future of Working-at-Height Safety Is Connected, Not Just Automated
Construction safety is moving toward a more connected operating model in which risk assessments, field inspections, permit approvals, contractor records and management information can work together.

AI may help identify patterns that are difficult to recognise manually. Digital workflows can improve coordination and accountability. Engineering controls and competent supervision remain the foundation of protection.

For construction and EPC organisations, the opportunity is not simply to collect more safety data.

It is to turn reliable information into timely preventive action.

